          "question": "Burning when I pee and going more often since yesterday, no temperature.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine.** The fixed red-flag table found no emergency pattern in what you described. That is not a clearance. If anything below appears, get medical help the same day, or call emergency services if you feel very unwell.\n\n**Seek urgent care now if you develop any of these:**\n- Fever, chills, or shaking\n- Pain in your side or back (flank pain)\n- Nausea or vomiting, or being unable to keep fluids down\n- Feeling much more unwell than usual, confusion, or unusual drowsiness\n- Blood in your urine. The guidance says this is not by itself a sign of something worse, but it should be checked.\n\nIf you might be pregnant, tell a clinician soon. The guidance treats infections in pregnancy on a different pathway.\n\n**Body systems involved:** Your symptoms fall in one system, the urinary tract. The tool's only other match was \"fever,\" which you said you don't have, so I have not counted it. The retrieved guidance describes a lower urinary tract infection, a bladder infection, as one common cause of burning and frequent urination (Urinary tract infection reference; ICD-10 symptom chapter). Only a clinician can tell whether that applies to you. The other conditions the tool listed (reflux, flu, insomnia, asthma) do not match your symptoms, so I have left them out.\n\n**What to discuss with a clinician:** Burning and frequency that started yesterday are worth a routine appointment, usually within a day or two. Tell them about any red flags above, whether you might be pregnant, and any medicines you already take. The guidance I retrieved does not cover which treatment is right for you, so please ask your clinician or pharmacist about that.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
